      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that a line is parameterized by a parameter \\( t \\), the vector on the line at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), and the vector on the line at \\( t = 3 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\).\n\nFirst, we observe that the solution geometry can be separated into two coordinate vector components: the path along the \\( x \\)-axis and the path along the \\( y \\)-axis.\n\n1. Path along the \\( x \\)-axis:\nThe direction along the \\( x \\)-axis is determined by the slope \\( \\frac{v_2 - v_1}{t_2 - t_1} \\). From \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = 3 \\):\n\\[\n\\text{slope } = \\frac{v_3 - v_1}{t_3 - t_1} = \\frac{-4 - 4}{3 - 2} = \\frac{-8}{1} = -8\n\\]\nThe \\( x \\)-coordinate potentially increases by the number of unit steps covered along the \\( x \\)-axis:\n\\[\nx_{\\text{initial}} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\nx_{\\text{final}} = 1 + (3 - 2) \\cdot -8 = 1 + 1 \\cdot -8 = 1 - 8 = -7\n\\]\nSo, at \\( t = -7 \\), the \\( x \\)-coordinate is:\n\\[\nx_{(-7)} = -7\n\\]\n\n2. Path along the \\( y \\)-axis:\nThe direction along the \\( y \\)-axis is determined by the slope \\( \\frac{v_2 - v_1}{t_2 - t_1} \\). From \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = 3 \\):\n\\[\n\\text{slope } = \\frac{v_3 - v_1}{t_3 - t_1} = \\frac{-4 - 4}{3 - 2} = \\frac{-8}{1} = -8\n\\]\nThe \\( y \\)-coordinate potentially increases by the number of unit steps covered along the \\( y \\)-axis:\n\\[\ny_{\\text{initial}} = 4\n\\]\n\\[\ny_{\\text{final}} = 4 + (3 - 2) \\cdot -8 = 4 + 1 \\cdot -8 = 4 - 8 = -4\n\\]\nSo, at \\( t = -7 \\), the \\( y \\)-coordinate is:\n\\[\ny_{(-7)} = -4\n\\]\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThe answer is:\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}}.",
